{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the city fully wakes up. The streets are empty enough to feel borrowed, yet alive enough to promise that something ordinary and wonderful might happen before breakfast. I walk without headphones then, because the world provides a better soundtrack than anything I could carefully choose for myself on purpose. There is the soft hiss of buses, the clatter of shutters, the distant argument of gulls, and somebody laughing behind a closed bakery door. In that light, even familiar buildings look briefly honest, as if they have forgotten the performances they maintain for crowds throughout the day. I think we all need versions of that hour in our lives, spaces where we are not explaining, competing, improving, or being endlessly watched. Maybe it is a garden, a workshop, a notebook, a long drive, or simply a chair by a window with no phone nearby. What matters is not productivity, but permission to exist without turning every feeling into a project and every silence into an uncomfortable problem. We return from those quiet places slightly kinder, I suspect, because we remember that other people also carry invisible mornings inside them too. They also carry worries they have not named, hopes they are afraid to say aloud, and small joys nobody else would understand fully. So I try to greet the day gently, offer patience generously, and leave a little unclaimed wonder waiting patiently for whoever needs it most tomorrow at the very next sunrise.",
